4.25 hr - Live lecture (morning): Terraform state lock, S3 backend and interview questions
          - Git + Terraform flow: clone/pull -> new branch -> plan -> apply ->
            push -> PR -> merge -> pull; why git pull before every change
          - Drift = manual change on real infra; stale code shows reverse change
          - State lock: two users hitting at once cause conflict, so Terraform
            locks state; lock applies on both plan and apply
          - Lock info: ID, path, who, created time (UTC); stale lock stays forever
            if process was killed (Ctrl+Z) instead of graceful exit (Ctrl+C)
          - terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID>, or delete the lock entry directly;
            -lock=false bypasses the lock (not recommended)
          - S3 backend: bucket, key (folder path inside bucket), region
          - DynamoDB table for locking: partition key must be LockID (capital L,
            capital ID) else "missing the key LockID" error
          - S3 native locking: use_lockfile = true, creates a .tflock object next
            to the state file, saves DynamoDB cost
          - terraform init -migrate-state (local to remote) vs -reconfigure
            (any backend change)
          - init downloads provider dependencies, creates .terraform/ and
            .terraform.lock.hcl (provider version lock, not Terraform version)
          - Providers are the "dictionary"; resource block needs its provider
          - terraform fmt (indentation), validate (syntax/arguments)
          - Flow: init -> fmt -> validate -> plan -> apply (-> destroy)
          - State file deleted: if versioning enabled, delete the delete-marker;
            else re-create with terraform import (resource block first, then
            terraform import <resource>.<name> <id>); one resource at a time
          - terraform state list, terraform state show
          - Modules: root, child, remote (git source with ref branch/tag)
          - Workspaces vs Terragrunt (Terragrunt is the better answer)
          - Provisioners: local-exec, remote-exec, file
          - null_resource with triggers and depends_on
          - lifecycle: create_before_destroy, prevent_destroy, ignore_changes,
            replace_triggered_by
          - terraform taint / untaint (new way: apply -replace=<resource>)
          - count vs for_each; each.key / each.value
          - Sensitive data: sensitive = true, Vault, AWS Parameter Store + data block
          - terraform graph (resource dependency graph)
          - Interview tips: interviews check whether you are genuine, not just
            knowledge; give answers AI cannot give; teach your work to others so
            you learn new skills

